WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.868
[MUSIC - ROBERT ASHBRIDGE,
 "GOING UP"]

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.020
Not commenting code.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.610
Oh my god.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.680
So when I was an
undergrad, I once

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.780
tried to explain to
one of my professors,

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.750
who was one of the
original authors of Java,

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.420
that Python was
self-documenting.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.540
And so I didn't need to
comment my code because Python

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.620
is so easy to read.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:27.820
And he just laughed at me.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.930
He laughed for five minutes and
was like, get out of my office.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.580
Maybe it's one indexing.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.700
One indexing is a pretty
awful language decision

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.330
that I really
vehemently dislike.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.950
Repetitive code.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.230
Nobody wants to see
the same code written

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.480
1,000 times in a file.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.100
Long code.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.670
People reinventing the same
classes over and over again.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:55.000
This is very commonplace
in the Python and PyTorch

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.330
community, that people are
reinventing the PyTorch

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.070
layers over and over again.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.030
And nobody wants to see that.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.360
And everyone just wants
to use the standard ones.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.383
But for some
reason, nobody does.

00:01:07.383 --> 00:01:08.800
Maybe it's not
really a pet peeve.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.160
A source of misery is
debugging distributed things.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.180
So when you have multiple
nodes working together

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.010
and something's
breaking somewhere,

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:20.080
debugging stack traces of
where that's happening,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.270
and reasoning across
multiple independent servers,

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.570
and then all these race
conditions is suffering.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.060
I really don't
like it when people

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.850
write large blocks of code
and no breakups of creating

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:34.900
functions and everything.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.650
It's like, modularization
is so useful

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:43.810
because it helps break up little
concepts into a format that's

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.240
actually easy to
understand for people.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.600
And also, lack of documentation.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.760
Well, that would be too
a pet peeve of mine.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.160
I think my biggest pet peeve
would be lack of organization.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:00.320
So I guess particularly in
file layout and code structure.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.510
So I think, yeah,
sometimes you see

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.970
either just a bunch of
different pieces of code

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:08.500
all in one directory.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.880
And there's no directory layout.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.340
Or even within a
given piece of code,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.580
there's, yeah, just not
much rhyme or reason to

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.140
how the structure is laid out.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.760
Sometimes, it's
just like the code--

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.820
you can clearly tell if the code
evolved as someone was thinking

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.530
and there wasn't
much restructuring.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.770
And so, yeah, I think,
yeah, lack of organization

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:30.640
makes it really hard to go back
and either as a third party

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.332
understand what someone was
doing or even yourself a year

00:02:34.332 --> 00:02:36.040
later trying to
understand your own code.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.840
It's almost like a
different person.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.030
Poor documentation.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.220
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.050
Lack of comments and
poor documentation.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.460
Lack of explanation.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.710
Lack of documentation
around APIs.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.130
And yeah, make it
easy for me, please.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.380
[MUSIC - ROBERT ASHBRIDGE,
 "GOING UP"]

